4d. There's no CommandSource abstraction — and you're about to need one
You said:

Definitely want to have like the modularity to be able to swap in VR teleop, or just keyboard teleop, I wanna add those motion primitives to be able to liek , keyboard teleop pick up a box lift it drop it.

Right now, every command source mutates control_dict directly:

Keyboard listener (gear_wbc_teleop.start_gear_wbc_teleop_listener) — pynput thread, mutates under cmd_lock.
Legacy UDP receiver (legacy_vr_code.udp_teleop_receiver.start_udp_receiver_for_runtime) — UDP thread, apply_udp_packet_to_control_dict.
New VR stream (vr_teleop.openvr_stream.OpenvrStreamUdpReceiver) — UDP thread, apply_vr_stream_locomotion + apply_vr_stream_ik_targets from inside step_physics.
OpenVR (openvr_teleop external module that may or may not exist) — its own poller.
Each lives in a different place, has a different lifecycle, and has its own way of applying values. step_physics has a hard-coded order:


gear_wbc_stand.py
Lines 504-511
        vr_snap, vr_t_rx = snapshot_vr_receiver(self)
        apply_vr_stream_locomotion(self, vr_snap, vr_t_rx)
        sync_keyboard_loco_hold(self, vr_snap, vr_t_rx)
        vr_ik_trace = apply_vr_stream_ik_targets(self, vr_snap, vr_t_rx)
That's fine now, but adding "motion primitive driving the arms while keyboard drives the legs while VR is idle" or "GR00T policy outputs navigate_command while keyboard reset key still works" turns this into a tangle of if-priorities very fast.

Proposal — define a tiny CommandSource protocol, e.g.:

class CommandSource(Protocol):
    name: str  # "keyboard", "vr_stream", "udp_v1", "primitive:pick_box", "policy:gr00t"
    owns: frozenset[str]  # which control_dict keys this source writes
    priority: int
    def start(self, rt: GearWBCRuntime) -> None: ...
    def stop(self) -> None: ...
    def apply(self, rt: GearWBCRuntime) -> dict[str, Any]:  # returns a per-step trace dict
        ...
Then GearWBCRuntime keeps an ordered list of sources and each step_physics is for src in self.sources: src.apply(self) plus a tiny conflict policy ("higher priority wins on overlapping keys; unowned keys fall through"). Concrete benefits:

VR vs keyboard swap is --teleop {keyboard, vr, vr+keyboard, none} — one flag, no wall of validation.
Motion primitives ("pick up the box") become a CommandSource that runs its own state machine, owns ee_*_pos for some seconds, then yields back. Same protocol as keyboard — they automatically compose.
GR00T inference is one more CommandSource that owns arm_target_q + loco_cmd.
The agent layer ("agentic stuff") is a meta-source that picks which source is active — trivial to wire later.
That's 50–80 lines of new code plus moving the existing 4 sources to obey it. It's the single highest-leverage refactor for your stated future goals.

